  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "intro": "Five positions from the lines you have just learnt, each stopping on the move that matters. Play through the moves and find the last one.\n\n## What these are\n\nTwo of them end on a capture that wins a piece, two end on a choice between a sacrifice and something better, and one ends on the quiet move that starts the attack. All five come from the same tabiya, because that is what a system opening is: one position asked over and over.\n\n## The question behind three of them\n\nWhat does the pawn on e5 attack after I recapture.\n\nThe knight on e5 is the point of the whole setup, and the moment Black takes it, a PAWN arrives on e5. That pawn attacks d6 and f6. If Black has a bishop on d6 and a knight on f6, it attacks both and one of them is lost. Count that before you allow the trade and take it the moment Black allows it. It happens with dxe5 when f4 has not been played and with fxe5 when it has, and it is the single most common way this opening wins a game.\n\n## The Greek gift, honestly\n\nBxh7+ is the tactic the Colle is famous for, and in this repertoire it is usually not there. It needs a knight that can reach g5 with check or a rook already on the third rank, and the Zukertort's knight is committed to e5. Two of the five exercises are about resisting it: in one the sacrifice works and a plain capture wins twice as much, and in the other it simply fails.\n\nThat is not a reason to forget the pattern. It is a reason to check it. If your knight cannot follow the bishop and your rook is not on the third rank, the sacrifice is a hope, not a combination.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nRecapturing with a piece when the pawn forks two of them. Sacrificing on h7 because the position looks like a Colle. Taking on e4 with the bishop when the knight should take. And starting the rook lift before f4 has opened the file it needs.",
